WEBVTT

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:05.661
Let's collect several common mistakes in one place
First mixing

00:00:05.674 --> 00:00:08.806
OR and AND without parentheses can change the
meaning of the rule

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:13.189
Second comparing a field to null with equals does
not work as

00:00:13.214 --> 00:00:18.382
a missing-value test Third NOT IN becomes dangerous
when the list itself

00:00:18.395 --> 00:00:24.104
contains null Fourth ordinary not-equal comparisons
can silently exclude records

00:00:24.129 --> 00:00:29.347
where the value is null Finally putting a broad case
condition before

00:00:29.373 --> 00:00:34.297
a more specific condition can make the specific
branch unreachable Notice that

00:00:34.323 --> 00:00:39.606
most of these queries are syntactically valid
PostgreSQL may run them without

00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:43.975
displaying an error That is why testing SQL is not
only about

00:00:43.988 --> 00:00:47.982
asking whether the query runs We also need to ask
whether the

00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:49.864
result matches the intended rule
